The diagnostics suite runs synthetic files in a temporary mailbox. It verifies that the production publisher assigns the requester as file owner and applies a protected explicit DACL containing only requester and SYSTEM full-control ACEs. It also exercises bootstrap argument failures, source tampering, installer preflight and staged failures, bootstrap exceptions, nonce and record corruption, inaccessible/missing/malformed diagnostics, child exit conflicts, UAC cancellation behavior, and false success claims. No UAC, protected installation, live host query, or filesystem permission change was performed.

PR #54 Windows CI history before the owner/DACL correction:
- Run 38175626017 failed the new diagnostic transport suite: 18 assertions failed because elevated-runner-created diagnostic files had an unexpected owner and inherited rather than protected explicit DACL. The directory check passed.
- Run 38175878414 reproduced that same synthetic mailbox file owner/DACL mismatch. The failure report was sanitized to owner-match/protection booleans, ACE categories, masks and flags; it contained no private SID or raw SDDL.
- Correction: the bootstrap now creates the diagnostic file with an explicit protected SYSTEM/requester DACL and requester owner, flushes the create-new file, verifies its security descriptor, then publishes by same-directory non-overwriting move. The requester requires the same owner/protected-DACL contract.
- The final GitHub Windows CI result for the immutable commit containing this log is linked in the PR #54 review handoff; the source workflow runs the same five suites, parser sweep, and whitespace check.

